legal     2f       bits 00101111
overlong  c0 af    bits 00000 101111
the payload of both, as a number: 47 and 47
Same value. A decoder that just assembles the bits gets '/' from both.

Python: UnicodeDecodeError -- refused, and it is right to

WHY REFUSING IS NOT PEDANTRY

The moment one character has two byte strings, a check that reads BYTES
and a step that reads CHARACTERS can be made to disagree -- and they
will disagree in the attacker's favour, because the check runs first.

   a filter looks for the byte 2f              -- the path separator
   the request carries c0 af                   -- not 2f, filter passes
   a lenient decoder later yields '/'          -- and the path escapes

That is not hypothetical: it is the shape of the IIS directory-traversal
bug, and it is why RFC 3629 made the shortest form a REQUIREMENT rather
than a recommendation. One character, one spelling, so the two readings
cannot come apart.

THE RULE, AS ARITHMETIC
   U+002F first needs 1 byte(s); anything longer for this code point is overlong
   U+0080 first needs 2 byte(s); anything longer for this code point is overlong
   U+0800 first needs 3 byte(s); anything longer for this code point is overlong
   U+10000 first needs 4 byte(s); anything longer for this code point is overlong
   So each length has a minimum code point, and a sequence is legal only
   if its value reaches its own length's floor. Two bytes must carry at
   least U+0080; c0 and c1 can never begin a legal sequence at all,
   which is why they appear nowhere in valid UTF-8.

AND THE SAME TRAP WITHOUT THE ENCODING
   canonicalize, THEN check. Any time a value has more than one
   representation -- overlong UTF-8, %2f in a URL, a Windows short
   filename, a trailing dot -- a check that runs before normalization is
   checking a different string from the one that gets used.
